<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Gangut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1714">1714</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Hangö, Battle of Hanko · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Gangut, fought off the Hanko Peninsula of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> on 7 August 1714 (27 July Old Style), was the first major victory of the Russian navy. Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> under Fyodor Apraksin, with Peter I present, outmanoeuvred a Swedish squadron left becalmed and captured its flagship and several smaller vessels. The victory secured Russian control of the Finnish coast during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gangut.jpg" alt="Battle of Gangut" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Gangut-Bakua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Peter I had begun his offensive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in the spring of 1713, and the Russian armies advanced to Åbo (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku">Turku</a>). After the Russian victory at the Battle of Storkyro on 19 February 1714 southern Finland was fully in Russian hands. The Russian governor, Prince Mikhail Galitzine, could not be supplied by sea, because a Swedish fleet under Admiral Gustav Wattrang had blockaded the coastal route past the Hanko Peninsula since 24 April. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> also kept the Russian coastal fleet from raiding the Swedish coast.</p><p>Admiral Apraksin brought a galley fleet of 80 ships to the peninsula on 29 June 1714 and met 16 ships of the line and seven smaller ships. He withdrew to the eastern side and called for reinforcements. Peter I, finding his battle fleet ill-prepared, abandoned its use and reached the coastal fleet on 20 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A first attempt to haul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> across the peninsula on ox skins was abandoned after the second galley was damaged. A second attempt used calm weather on the morning of 26 July: the small galleys were easily manoeuvred, while the heavy Swedish ships were hard to turn. Apraksin sent 20 galleys past the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, then 15 more, and on 27 July the Russians ran the cleared sea route along the cape.</p><p>Only the small coastal squadron of Rear Admiral Nils Ehrenskiöld then stood in their way. He formed a defensive line between two islands, with the pram Elefanten broadside-on. After he refused to surrender, the Russians attacked at 14:00 on 27 July 1714. They boarded the galley Tranan, which capsized, opened a gap in the line and boarded the remaining ships. Fighting lasted three hours and ended with the capture of Elefanten and Ehrenskiöld himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The loss of the small squadron mattered less than the fact that over 70 Russian galleys had passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> and reached the Archipelago Sea. Apraksin sailed from Rilax on 1 August, reached Åland by 8 August and had the whole of Åland under Russian control on 13 August. Russian galleys razed the Swedish town of Umeå on 18 September, and the Swedish army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> withdrew to the Torne River.</p><p>Swedish accounts of repeated Russian attacks and heavy Russian losses were disputed in 1996 by the Russian historian Paul A. Krotov, who found one massive attack and 127 men killed and 347 wounded. The victory is commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as a Day of Military Honour, and a stone cross at Riilahti manor, which replaced a wooden one of 1869, commemorates it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Gangut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
